      "claim": "Francesco Redi (1668) disproved the macroscopic aspect (flies from meat) using controlled jars with and without gauze covers, without the use of a microscope.",
      "evidence": "Redi's experiment showed maggots only appeared in uncovered meat, proving they came from fly eggs, not the meat itself.",
      "weight": 0.3,
      "depends_on": [1]
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "claim": "Louis Pasteur (1859) later disproved the microscopic aspect using swan-neck flasks, relying on experimental design (sealed vs. open) rather than just observation.",
      "evidence": "Pasteur's broth remained sterile in swan-neck flasks despite air exposure, proving microbes come from other microbes, not spontaneous generation.",
      "weight": 0.3,
      "depends_on": [1]
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "claim": "The primary method of disproof was controlled experimentation (manipulating variables like access to air/organisms), not the invention of a specific tool like the microscope.",
      "evidence": "Redi's work predates the practical use of microscopes for this purpose and relied on macroscopic observation; the core scientific contribution was the experimental design.",
      "weight": 0.2,
      "depends_on": [2, 3]
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "claim": "Therefore, the idea was disproved primarily because of continued experimentation.",
      "evidence": "Option C directly describes the methodological process (Redi and Pasteur's experiments) that refuted the hypothesis.",
